From: rtw89-usb-ap-fix <local@patch>
Date: Sun, 7 Sep 2026 16:40:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] rtw89: reset RTL8922AU out of high-speed enumeration

RTL8922AU on Raspberry Pi 4 (VL805 xHCI + VIA hub) enumerates at
high-speed first through the hub's USB2 path. The chip's PAD_CTRL2
MATCH_CNT field already equals USB_SWITCH_DELAY out of power-on, so
rtw89_usb_switch_mode_be() believes a USB3 switch was attempted and
returns 0 without doing anything, and - because every vendor request
fails with -71 on this platform - the driver cannot request the U3
switch itself either.

A full probe is then run over USB2 and dies in -71 errors. The probe
also burns 18-40 seconds per attempt waiting for versioned firmware
files (rtw8922a_fw-4..1.bin) that do not exist, falling back to the
sysfs loader each time. The failure disconnects the device, the dongle
resets, re-enumerates as the DISK (0bda:1a2b) CD-ROM, switches back to
WLAN mode, and the cycle repeats. On iStoreOS this burns ~90 seconds
per boot, during which the USB activity knocks the on-board bcmgenet
GbE link down (WAN DHCP fails and the dongle has to be unplugged to
boot cleanly), and netifd gives up on the wifi radio before the phy
ever registers.

The re-enumeration loop is the only mechanism that eventually gets the
dongle onto the SuperSpeed bus, where it works fine. Accelerate it:
when an RTL8922A enumerates below SuperSpeed, reset the device up to
five times in a row, checking the negotiated speed after each port
reset. A reset that lands on the SuperSpeed bus lets probe continue
normally on a working link. On a USB2-only bus (e.g. the dongle sits
behind a USB2 hub) every reset still enumerates at high-speed; after
five attempts probe continues anyway, preserving the stock
probe/switch behaviour instead of leaving the device without a driver
(note: usb_reset_device() re-binds in place, so a probe that bails out
after a reset would leave the device stuck unbound). A module-level
counter caps the resets at 25 across re-enumerations, after which the
stock behaviour is used permanently.

Users who disable USB3 switching with switch_usb_mode=N keep the old
behaviour.
---
diff --git a/usb.c b/usb.c
index 1399439..4122e26 100644
--- a/usb.c
+++ b/usb.c
@@ -14,6 +14,8 @@ module_param_named(switch_usb_mode, rtw89_switch_usb_mode, bool, 0644);
 MODULE_PARM_DESC(switch_usb_mode,
 		 "Set to N to disable switching to USB 3 mode to avoid potential interference in the 2.4 GHz band (default: Y)");
 
+static atomic_t rtw89_hs_reset_count = ATOMIC_INIT(0);
+
 static void rtw89_usb_read_port_complete(struct urb *urb);
 
 static void rtw89_usb_vendorreq(struct rtw89_dev *rtwdev, u32 addr,
@@ -1203,6 +1205,28 @@ int rtw89_usb_probe(struct usb_interface *intf,
 		goto err_free_hw;
 	}
 
+	if (rtwdev->chip->chip_id == RTL8922A &&
+	    rtw89_switch_usb_mode &&
+	    atomic_read(&rtw89_hs_reset_count) < 25) {
+		int n;
+
+		for (n = 0; n < 5; n++) {
+			atomic_inc(&rtw89_hs_reset_count);
+			rtw89_info(rtwdev, "RTL8922A on high-speed bus, resetting for SuperSpeed re-enumeration (attempt %d/5)\n",
+				n + 1);
+			if (usb_reset_device(rtwusb->udev))
+				break;
+			msleep(200);
+			if (rtwusb->udev->speed == USB_SPEED_SUPER)
+				break;
+		}
+
+		if (rtwusb->udev->speed == USB_SPEED_SUPER)
+			rtw89_info(rtwdev, "RTL8922A re-enumerated at SuperSpeed, continuing probe\n");
+		else
+			rtw89_info(rtwdev, "RTL8922A still on high-speed bus, continuing probe anyway\n");
+	}
+
 	ret = rtw89_usb_switch_mode(rtwdev);
 	if (ret) {
 		/* Not a fail, but we do need to skip rtw89_core_register. */
